ptured run output is kept in `.devflow/`, which IS ignored (confirmed by `git check-ignore`), and `.devflow/` has never appeared in any ref.\n159\t**Fix:** reconcile or document intent.\n160\t\n161\t## `.gitignore` coverage check\n162\t\n163\t| Path written by tooling | Ignored? |\n164\t|---|---|\n165\t| `.devflow/` | yes (`.gitignore:1`) |\n166\t| `.worktrees/` | yes (`.gitignore:2`) |\n167\t| `__pycache__/`, `*.pyc` | yes (`.gitignore:3-4`); `probe/__pycache__` and `tests/__pycache__` present on disk and ignored |\n168\t| `.gsd/` | yes, via inner `.gitignore` only (IN-01) |\n169\t| `.planning/state.json` | NO (WR-01) |\n170\t\n171\t## Code-level review: `probe/budget.py` (deep)\n172\t\n173\t```\n174\tDEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120\n175\tdef deadline_after(start_secs: float) -> float: return start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS\n176\t```\n177\t- No I/O, no parsing, no eval/subprocess, no env or file access, no secrets: no injection or traversal surface.\n178\t- No input validation: `deadline_after` accepts NaN, inf, negative, and non-numeric values (e.g. a str raises TypeError; NaN/inf silently propagate: `nan` makes `time.monotonic() < deadline` always False, so a retry loop exits immediately; `inf` makes it never expire). This only matters when a caller passes an untrusted or computed start time; there are no callers in the reviewed scope (`probe/__init__.py` and tests only), so this is unverified-impact, not a confirmed defect. Severity if reported: Info.\n179\t- Semantics: the function adds to whatever clock the caller supplies (wall vs monotonic is undocumented). Mixing `time.time()` and `time.monotonic()` would be a correctness bug in future callers; the docstring does not say which clock is expected. Info.\n180\t- The tests (`tests/test_budget.py:10-20`) pin the constant and sampled arithmetic; they do not cover NaN/inf, which is consistent with the stated acceptance in 01-VALIDATION.md.\n181\t\n182\t## What this scan does not establish\n183\t\n184\t- It does not cover refs other than main..HEAD or content that exists only in the working tree's ignored files (`.gsd/dispatch-isolation-sentinel.json` and `__pycache__` bytecode were not read).\n185\t- gitleaks and rg find patterns; they cannot detect an unusual credential format with low entropy and no keyword. I read the full commit-message bodies and every rg hit by hand, but I did not line-read all ~2,400 lines of patch text.\n186\t- Remote state (what is already pushed or visible on a remote) was not checked.\n187\t=== ci\n188\t# CI / build correctness review (angle: can a failing step still report green?)\n189\t\n190\tWorktree: .../project/.worktrees/phase-01, branch feature/phase-01. Python 3.15.2 default. Depth: deep. Repository files not modified; scratch copies were deleted.\n191\t\n192\t## 1. What CI / build / hook configuration actually exists\n193\t\n194\tConfirmed:\n195\t- No `.github/`, `.githooks/`, Makefile, tox/nox, pyproject.toml, setup.cfg, pytest.ini, requirements*, or packaging manifest. `git ls-files` on this branch is only .gitignore, devflow.toml, .planning/*, probe/__init__.py, probe/budget.py, tests/test_budget.py. `git ls-tree -r` of origin/main and origin/integration show the same: no workflow files. No GitHub CI exists on this branch or on origin (checked trees only; GitHub-side settings, rulesets, required checks cannot be inspected from here: unverified).\n196\t- Repo-local git hooks: `<common-git-dir>/hooks` contains only `.sample` files (zero non-sample hooks).\n197\t- A hook does run, but it is global and not in the repo: `core.hooksPath=~/.config/git/hooks`, `pre-commit` = gitleaks secret scan, then chain to the repo's own pre-commit (none exists). It does not run tests. A fresh clone on another machine has no hook at all.\n198\t- Test config: none. `tests/` has no `__init__.py`; `probe/__init__.py` exists. Framework is stdlib unittest.\n199\t- Consequence: the only test gate is a human/agent running the command by hand. The plan (01-01-PLAN.md:67), 01-VALIDATION.md:22-23,30-31,41 and 01-VERIFICATION.md:42 all name `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v`.\n200\t\n201\t## 2. Test-command behaviour (exit codes read directly, not through a pipe; scratch copy, Python 3.15.2 unless noted)\n202\t\n203\t| Command | Baseline | Wrong constant (999) | Syntax error in budget.py | budget.py deleted | tests renamed (zero tests) |\n204\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n205\t| `python3 -m unittest` | 5, \"Ran 0 tests / NO TESTS RAN\" | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 |\n206\t| `python3 -m unittest discover` | 5, Ran 0 tests | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 |\n207\t| `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` (the documented one) | 0, Ran 2 tests, OK | 1 (FAILED failures=1) | 1 (errors=1) | 1 (errors=1) | 5 |\n208\t| `python3 -m unittest tests.test_budget` | 0, Ran 2 | 1 | 1 (SyntaxError) | 1 | 1 (import error) |\n209\t| `python3 -m unittest tests/test_budget.py` | 0, Ran 2 | not run | not run | not run | not run |\n210\t\n211\tFurther negative controls on `discover -s tests`: constant 30 (an \"offered\" value) exit 1; sign flip `start - BUDGET` exit 1; run from cwd `/` with `-s <abs>/tests` exit 1 (cannot import `probe`; fails loudly, not green).\n212\t\n213\tConclusion: the documented command correctly goes non-zero for every mutation tried, on Python 3.15.2. Bare `python3 -m unittest` and `python3 -m unittest discover` collect zero tests and fail (exit 5) here, so they are not silently green on this interpreter. The reason tests/ lacks `__init__.py` matters: bare discover starts at cwd, finds no `test*.py` at top level and no package in `tests/`, hence zero tests; only `-s tests` finds them.\n214\t\n215\t## Findings\n216\t\n217\t### WR-01 (Warning): zero-test collection exits 0 on Python < 3.12\n218\t- File: 01-01-PLAN.md:67, 01-VALIDATION.md:22-23 (the command), tests/ (no `__init__.py`)\n219\t- Command: Python 3.11.16, tests file renamed to `x.py`: `python3.11 -m unittest discover -s tests` -> exit 0, \"Ran 0 tests ... OK\". Bare `python3.11 -m unittest` -> exit 0, \"Ran 0 tests ... OK\". Same commands on 3.15.2 -> exit 5. (Exit 5 for NO TESTS RAN arrived in 3.12.)\n220\t- Scenario: the repo declares no Python version and has no CI. If the gate is run under 3.11 or earlier (mise has 3.11.16 installed on this host), a renamed/moved/mis-named test file, or a bare `python3 -m unittest`, reports OK with 0 tests. The documented command's \"Ran 2 tests\" assertion lives only in prose, nothing enforces it. Not Critical: no failure ships green today on the default interpreter, and no such rename exists on this branch.\n221\t- Fix: declare a minimum Python (>= 3.12) in a manifest, or have the gate fail on \"Ran 0 tests\" (e.g. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v 2>&1 | tee out; test ${PIPESTATUS[0]} -eq 0 && ! grep -q \"^Ran 0 tests\" out`).\n222\t\n223\t### WR-02 (Warning): no automated gate exists; \"green\" is an assertion in prose\n224\t- File: 01-VALIDATION.md:30-32,98-99; 01-VERIFICATION.md:42\n225\t- Evidence: no workflow, no repo hook, no manifest (section 1). The global pre-commit hook runs gitleaks only.\n226\t- Scenario: a later commit that breaks `probe/budget.py` (my controls show it would exit 1 if anyone ran the command) can still be committed, pushed and merged to main/integration with nothing failing. Nothing in this branch's code is broken today; this is a missing control, not a present defect. (Whether GitHub branch protection requires anything is unverifiable from here.)\n227\t- Fix: add a minimal workflow (`python-version: [\"3.12\",\"3.13\"]`, run `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` with no trailing pipe) or a repo-tracked hook, and pin the Python version.\n228\t\n229\t### WR-03 (Warning): verification command is pipe-fragile if wrapped\n230\t- File: 01-VERIFICATION.md:42 (`python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -v`), 01-01-PLAN.md:70\n231\t- Command: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v 2>&1 | tail -1` with a broken constant: the unittest exit was 1 (via `${PIPESTATUS[0]}`) but `$?` after the pipe was 0.\n232\t- Scenario: the documents only name the bare command (fine). Anyone wiring it into a script/CI step as `... | tail`/`| tee` without `set -o pipefail` gets green on a failing suite. Latent only; no such wrapper exists in the repo.\n233\t- Fix: when scripting, use `set -o pipefail` or capture `$?` before piping.\n234\t\n235\t### IN-01 (Info): devflow.toml `base_branch = \"integration\"` vs .planning/config.json `git.base_branch = \"main\"`\n236\t- File: devflow.toml:1; .planning/config.json:33-36 (`git.base_branch: \"main\"`, `branching_strategy: \"none\"`)\n237\t- Facts: devflow.toml exists only on origin/integration (commit 8cc4245 \"chore: configure devflow base branch\", introduced on `integration`; main is an ancestor of integration) and on this branch, not on origin/main. origin/main = 5b9fb44, origin/integration = 8cc4245 (one commit ahead). The `diff_base: main` given for this review means `git diff main` shows devflow.toml as an added file (+2 lines) which is an artifact of base choice.\n238\t- What could go wrong: two tools read two different base branches. A PR/merge flow driven by devflow targets `integration`; GSD (`/gsd-ship`, diff scoping, `gsd-pr-branch`) targets `main`. A phase branch merged to main bypasses integration, or a review scoped against main includes integration-only commits (such as devflow.toml itself). Neither tool is a CI gate here, so this cannot make a failing step green by itself; I did not run devflow (unknown behaviour of that tool: unverified).\n239\t- Fix: make GSD `git.base_branch` agree with devflow (`\"integration\"`) or document that devflow owns branching and GSD config is advisory; do not leave both set to different values.\n240\t\n241\t### IN-02 (Info): `.gitignore` covers `__pycache__/` and `*.pyc`, `.devflow/`, `.worktrees/`\n242\t- File: .gitignore:1-4\n243\t- No defect. Noted because `.planning/state.json` is untracked and not ignored (git status `??`), unrelated to CI.\n244\t\n245\t## Not found / not applicable\n246\t- No CI workflows, container builds, release/packaging pipelines, or lockfiles exist to evaluate. No Critical finding: I found no path on this branch where a failing step reports green today with the documented command on the default interpreter (3.15.2).\n247\t\n248\t## What these checks do not establish\n249\t- Only budget.py mutations (constant value, sign flip, syntax error, deletion, test-file rename) were tried; one run each, deterministic. Test strength against other mutations (for example constant 120 -> 121) was not probed here.\n250\t- Python 3.12-3.14 behaviour inferred from 3.15.2 and 3.11.16 results only; the 3.12 boundary is from memory of the changelog, not tested.\n251\t- GitHub-side protections and the devflow tool's actual use of base_branch were not inspected.\n252\t=== external-state\n253\t# External-state claims review (angle: external-state claims)\n254\t\n255\tScope: `git diff main...HEAD` on `feature/phase-01` (HEAD 02af1a3, 14 files, 35 commits). Review time 2026-11-02 19:33Z.\n256\tNothing was mutated; only read-only git/gh commands were used.\n257\t\n258\t## Reality (commands run)\n259\t\n260\t- `git ls-remote origin`: `HEAD` and `refs/heads/main` = 5b9fb44 (scaffold), `refs/heads/integration` = 8cc4245. No `feature/phase-01` on the remote.\n261\t- `git tag`: empty.\n262\t- `git branch -a`: feature/phase-01 (checked out), integration, main, remotes/origin/{integration,main}.\n263\t- `gh pr list --repo denniyahh/p501-live-attempt-2 --state all`: no output (no PRs).\n264\t- `gh run list --repo denniyahh/p501-live-attempt-2`: no output (no CI runs).\n265\t- `date -u` = 19:33:48Z; HEAD commit time = 19:31:51Z.\n266\t\n267\tNo file in the diff claims a merge, tag, release, push, PR, CI pass, or deletion. `rg -i 'merged|pushed|pull request|tag|released|shipped|CI |origin|deleted'` over `.planning` found only unrelated hits (\"tags:\" frontmatter, \"deleted afterwards\" in PROJECT.md describing the fixture's future). The false/misleading claims are all about completion and sign-off, below.\n268\t\n269\t## Findings\n270\t\n271\tNo Critical findings. The diff does not hide that no human confirmed the budget: UAT, VERIFICATION, SUMMARY, REVIEW-DISPOSITION and PROJECT.md Key Decisions all say \"orchestrator, not a human\".\n272\t\n273\t### WR-01 (Warning): VERIFICATION `verified` timestamp is in the future, and a round number\n274\t\n275\t`.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VERIFICATION.md:3, 30, 136`: `verified: 2026-11-02T20:00:00Z`, `**Verified:** 2026-11-02T20:00:00Z`, `_Verified: 2026-11-02T20:00:00Z_`.\n276\tContradiction: the commit that last touched the file (cad8fe6) is dated 14:30:49 -0500 = 19:30:49Z, and `date -u` at review time was 19:33:48Z. The claimed verification time is about 27 minutes after the last commit and still in the future at review time. The first version of this report (3e05607, 13:44:47 -0500 = 18:44:47Z) had the same pattern: `verified: 2026-11-02T19:00:00Z`, 15 minutes ahead of its own commit. A round-hour time is a typed placeholder, not a recorded event. Anyone ordering evidence by `verified:` gets the wrong sequence. The report's body says UAT sign-off (19:30:05Z) came first, which the timestamp does not contradict, but it is not a real measurement.\n277\t\n278\t### WR-02 (Warning): STATE.md, ROADMAP.md and REQUIREMENTS.md assert phase/requirement completion with no caveat, while the human item is open\n279\t\n280\t- `.planning/STATE.md:4, 21`: `status: completed`, `Status: All phases complete` (also `progress.percent: 100`, `Plan: Not started` at line 20).\n281\t- `.planning/ROADMAP.md:13, 15`: `1/1 plans complete`, `[x] 01-01-PLAN.md`.\n282\t- `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5, 12`: `[x] BUDGET-01`, `| BUDGET-01 | Phase 1 | Complete |`.\n283\t- `.planning/PROJECT.md:13-15`: `### Validated` with `✓ BUDGET-01 ... — Phase 1`.\n284\t\n285\tContradiction (same diff): `01-VERIFICATION.md:21-24, 115-128` lists `human_verification` as open (\"No human has confirmed the value\"; \"One item stays open for a human\"), and the plan's Task 1 is `checkpoint:decision gate=blocking-human` with `autonomous: false`. The git history shows STATE.md flipping `completed` -> `Phase 01 awaiting human verification` -> `completed` three times (e266cb1, 72e0715, ae58942, 8799d45, f1e8ab5). The final flip to `completed` (f1e8ab5, 14:30:19) came 14 seconds after the UAT file was marked complete (46954b9, 14:30:05), and the only decider recorded is the orchestrator. Of these four files only PROJECT.md (Key Decisions row, line 25) carries the \"no human has confirmed it\" caveat. Someone reading STATE/ROADMAP/REQUIREMENTS alone sees a finished, human-gated phase. The PROJECT.md \"Validated\" tick sits right above a row that says the value is unconfirmed. This is a completion claim on a blocking-human gate, disclosed elsewhere in the diff, so Warning rather than Critical.\n286\t\n287\t### WR-03 (Warning): \"Operator Decision\" in VALIDATION.md names an operator answer that nothing in the repo records, and conflicts with the UAT\n288\t\n289\t`.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VALIDATION.md:67-69`: \"The operator answered this audit's gap gate, recorded by the session running this live attempt: the samples ... close the gap ... this suite is enough for this throwaway fixture. Further mutation search ... is out of scope.\" Line 105: \"**Approval:** no open gaps at commit `7d8b545`, per the mutation recheck above and the Operator Decision recorded in this file.\" `01-REVIEW.md:29` repeats it (\"per the operator decision in `01-VALIDATION.md`\").\n290\tContradiction: `01-UAT.md:18, 23, 28` and `01-REVIEW-DISPOSITION.md:47` say the run had \"no operator available\" and that decisions were made by the orchestrator under a DevFlow resume message. The \"operator\" in VALIDATION.md is not named, not quoted, and its message is not stored in the repo (`rg -i operator .planning` shows no source). The text does not say whether this was a